1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:09,416
A narrow valley in central Norway, where glowing balls of light have floated over the hills for decades, up to twenty times a week.

2
00:00:09,416 --> 00:00:12,952
Scientists arrived with radar, cameras and a laser.

3
00:00:12,952 --> 00:00:15,933
In one test, a light seemed to answer back.

4
00:00:16,399 --> 00:00:24,881
The lights are real in the way that matters: they have been filmed, photographed and tracked on instruments for forty years.

5
00:00:24,881 --> 00:00:26,796
Some call them alien probes.

6
00:00:26,796 --> 00:00:28,233
Others say airplanes.

7
00:00:28,699 --> 00:00:34,490
Tonight we open the instrument rack and ask each device what it actually recorded.

8
00:00:34,490 --> 00:00:36,185
Only measurements count.

9
00:00:36,185 --> 00:00:38,233
This is the Hessdalen lights.

10
00:00:38,700 --> 00:00:40,090
The Hessdalen lights.

11
00:00:40,090 --> 00:00:41,680
Evidence against theory.

12
00:00:41,680 --> 00:00:43,733
Switch on the first instrument.

13
00:00:48,200 --> 00:00:50,387
December, nineteen eighty-one.

14
00:00:50,387 --> 00:00:56,000
Hessdalen, a valley about twelve kilometers long, north of the town of Røros.

15
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:01,833
Farmers see lights hanging over the hills, silent, white, yellow, sometimes red.

16
00:01:02,299 --> 00:01:04,045
Some flash for a second.

17
00:01:04,045 --> 00:01:06,445
Some hover for more than an hour.

18
00:01:06,445 --> 00:01:08,990
Some sway slowly from side to side.

19
00:01:08,990 --> 00:01:12,700
And some, witnesses say, shoot away at great speed.

20
00:01:13,166 --> 00:01:20,958
At the peak, between nineteen eighty-one and nineteen eighty-four, people reported them fifteen to twenty times a week.

21
00:01:20,958 --> 00:01:24,166
Tourists came just to stand in the cold and look.

22
00:01:24,633 --> 00:01:30,882
Locals had told stories of strange lights in these hills since at least the nineteen thirties.

23
00:01:30,882 --> 00:01:36,400
What changed in eighty-one was the number of witnesses, and the arrival of cameras.

24
00:01:36,866 --> 00:01:41,408
By then UFO clubs in Norway and Sweden were paying attention.

25
00:01:41,408 --> 00:01:50,566
In June nineteen eighty-three, five volunteers formed Project Hessdalen, led by an electrical engineer named Erling Strand.

26
00:01:51,033 --> 00:01:52,869
Their advisors included J.

27
00:01:52,869 --> 00:01:58,732
Allen Hynek, the astronomer who had worked on the US Air Force's Project Blue Book.

28
00:01:58,732 --> 00:02:05,866
In January nineteen eighty-four, they mailed report forms to three thousand three hundred households.

29
00:02:06,333 --> 00:02:13,215
Then they hauled a caravan up to a ridge called Aspåskjølen, filled it with instruments, and waited.

30
00:02:13,215 --> 00:02:20,166
Five weeks, from January twenty-first to February twenty-sixth, with volunteers on watch every night.

31
00:02:20,633 --> 00:02:25,907
Instrument one: a camera with a diffraction grating in front of the lens.

32
00:02:25,907 --> 00:02:28,219
It splits light into a spectrum.

33
00:02:28,219 --> 00:02:32,554
A glowing gas draws bright separate lines, like a neon sign.

34
00:02:32,554 --> 00:02:35,300
A hot solid glows in a smooth rainbow.

35
00:02:35,766 --> 00:02:39,291
Strand called this the most important measurement.

36
00:02:39,291 --> 00:02:42,322
Lines would mean glowing gas, maybe plasma.

37
00:02:42,322 --> 00:02:45,142
A smooth band would mean a thermal glow.

38
00:02:45,142 --> 00:02:48,666
Either answer would kill several theories at once.

39
00:02:49,133 --> 00:02:51,742
They shot seven films through gratings.

40
00:02:51,742 --> 00:02:56,156
Only four pictures were good enough to read: two strong, two weak.

41
00:02:56,156 --> 00:02:59,166
And none of them showed the strangest lights.

42
00:02:59,633 --> 00:03:01,411
Strand was strict about it.

43
00:03:01,411 --> 00:03:10,695
The best pictures, taken at eight minutes past three on the last morning, scored right on the borderline of his own scale for strange lights.

44
00:03:10,695 --> 00:03:13,000
His verdict: not proof of anything.

45
00:03:13,466 --> 00:03:15,357
Later campaigns did better.

46
00:03:15,357 --> 00:03:20,540
The spectrum of the white lights, recorded again and again, is continuous.

47
00:03:20,540 --> 00:03:21,660
No bright lines.

48
00:03:21,660 --> 00:03:27,333
That points to a hot, dense glow rather than a thin gas, and that is a real clue.

49
00:03:27,800 --> 00:03:34,359
One estimate puts the glow near five thousand degrees, a little cooler than the surface of the Sun.

50
00:03:34,359 --> 00:03:38,599
All of that from a ball of light floating over a Norwegian hill.

51
00:03:39,066 --> 00:03:41,797
What does the camera say about aliens?

52
00:03:41,797 --> 00:03:43,018
Nothing directly.

53
00:03:43,018 --> 00:03:47,545
A continuous spectrum fits burning dust, hot plasma, or a lamp.

54
00:03:47,545 --> 00:03:51,066
It tells us how it glows, not who or what lit it.

55
00:03:51,533 --> 00:03:53,048
Instrument two: radar.

56
00:03:53,048 --> 00:03:58,556
A set with a three-centimeter wavelength and a range of thirty-three kilometers.

57
00:03:58,556 --> 00:04:04,133
If a solid object, or a charged cloud, hung out there, the screen should show it.

58
00:04:04,599 --> 00:04:06,708
Thirty-six echoes were logged.

59
00:04:06,708 --> 00:04:09,871
Only three lined up with a light seen by eye.

60
00:04:09,871 --> 00:04:13,245
Nine could be followed in nearly straight lines.

61
00:04:13,245 --> 00:04:17,533
When the team photographed those spots, the sky looked empty.

62
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:21,330
Two of the echoes were photographed on screen.

63
00:04:21,330 --> 00:04:25,458
They were as strong as returns from the nearby mountains.

64
00:04:25,458 --> 00:04:33,133
A radar expert at the Norwegian defence research lab said: if it is only gas, it must be strongly ionized.

65
00:04:33,600 --> 00:04:34,687
One odd detail.

66
00:04:34,687 --> 00:04:41,504
A light stayed visible the whole time, yet its radar echo appeared only on every second sweep.

67
00:04:41,504 --> 00:04:45,347
Strand wondered whether it was reacting to the radar.

68
00:04:45,347 --> 00:04:47,233
A question, not a finding.

69
00:04:47,699 --> 00:04:49,703
Radar has a known weak spot.

70
00:04:49,703 --> 00:04:56,717
Layers of different temperature and humidity can bounce a beam and make echoes from nothing solid.

71
00:04:56,717 --> 00:04:58,649
Strand listed that himself.

72
00:04:58,649 --> 00:05:02,300
The echoes are real data, but their source is open.

73
00:05:02,766 --> 00:05:08,099
Instrument three: a seismograph, installed in October nineteen eighty-three.

74
00:05:08,099 --> 00:05:13,782
One idea said the lights were earthquake lights, squeezed from rock under strain.

75
00:05:13,782 --> 00:05:18,133
It recorded no local tremors at all, only distant earthquakes.

76
00:05:18,600 --> 00:05:24,877
The Geiger counters heard nothing unusual, though no light came within a kilometer of them.

77
00:05:24,877 --> 00:05:29,706
The infrared viewer, used twice on far-off lights, saw nothing strong.

78
00:05:29,706 --> 00:05:31,500
Neither test settles much.

79
00:05:31,966 --> 00:05:41,029
The magnetometer showed a tantalizing forty percent link: four of ten bright sightings coincided with a magnetic pulsation.

80
00:05:41,029 --> 00:05:45,450
But pulsations were frequent that week, so chance is likely.

81
00:05:45,450 --> 00:05:47,366
Strand wrote exactly that.

82
00:05:47,833 --> 00:05:51,240
And a detail that rarely reaches documentaries.

83
00:05:51,240 --> 00:05:56,603
The cameras failed repeatedly, often at the moments they were needed most.

84
00:05:56,603 --> 00:05:58,778
Power connections dropped out.

85
00:05:58,778 --> 00:06:03,199
Fieldwork in a Norwegian winter is hard on fragile equipment.

86
00:06:03,666 --> 00:06:05,914
Instrument four is the strangest.

87
00:06:05,914 --> 00:06:09,662
Locals said a light vanished when a bright beam hit it.

88
00:06:09,662 --> 00:06:17,633
So the team brought a red laser, less than one milliwatt, weaker than a common laser pointer, and a rule: no peeking.

89
00:06:18,100 --> 00:06:23,324
Sunday, February twelfth, nineteen eighty-four, seven thirty-five p.m.

90
00:06:23,324 --> 00:06:27,429
A light drifts north over the ridge, flashing steadily.

91
00:06:27,429 --> 00:06:30,041
One flash, pause, one flash, pause.

92
00:06:30,041 --> 00:06:33,399
For fifteen minutes the rhythm never changes.

93
00:06:33,866 --> 00:06:35,545
Strand aims the laser.

94
00:06:35,545 --> 00:06:40,124
At once the rhythm changes: flash-flash, pause, flash-flash.

95
00:06:40,124 --> 00:06:41,116
He lowers it.

96
00:06:41,116 --> 00:06:42,871
Back to single flashes.

97
00:06:42,871 --> 00:06:45,314
Ten seconds on, ten seconds off.

98
00:06:45,314 --> 00:06:48,366
Four cycles, until the light disappears.

99
00:06:48,833 --> 00:06:52,388
An hour later a second flashing light repeats it.

100
00:06:52,388 --> 00:06:54,492
In all they tried nine times.

101
00:06:54,492 --> 00:06:56,668
Eight times the light changed.

102
00:06:56,668 --> 00:07:00,223
Strand's reaction in the report: very surprising.

103
00:07:00,223 --> 00:07:02,400
He had expected a coincidence.

104
00:07:02,866 --> 00:07:04,930
The test was blind in one way.

105
00:07:04,930 --> 00:07:11,397
The man watching through binoculars, Kurt Persson, could not see where the laser was pointing.

106
00:07:11,397 --> 00:07:14,699
He simply called out the flashes as he saw them.

107
00:07:15,166 --> 00:07:16,442
What does it prove?

108
00:07:16,442 --> 00:07:20,538
Two lights, one night, no independent repeat that I can find.

109
00:07:20,538 --> 00:07:25,239
Strong enough to be intriguing, not strong enough to rest a theory on.

110
00:07:25,239 --> 00:07:29,066
It is the best moment for the alien idea, and it is thin.

111
00:07:29,533 --> 00:07:35,410
The believer's reading is simple: the lights are probes, and one noticed the laser.

112
00:07:35,410 --> 00:07:38,455
It is the only result that fits that story.

113
00:07:38,455 --> 00:07:40,934
But the change was a flash pattern.

114
00:07:40,934 --> 00:07:43,766
Nobody saw a surface, a hull or an edge.

115
00:07:44,233 --> 00:07:49,375
Strand's own conclusion, signed in January nineteen eighty-five, was sober.

116
00:07:49,375 --> 00:07:52,528
We have not found out what this phenomenon is.

117
00:07:52,528 --> 00:07:57,533
But whatever it is, it can be measured, by light, by radar, and by laser.

118
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:00,813
And the most honest number in the report.

119
00:08:00,813 --> 00:08:08,224
Of one hundred eighty-eight sightings, Strand ruled that one hundred thirty-five were ordinary known lights.

120
00:08:08,224 --> 00:08:12,066
Only fifty-three deserved the name Hessdalen phenomenon.

121
00:08:12,533 --> 00:08:19,801
Of those fifty-three, about two thirds moved along a north-south line, roughly the line of the valley.

122
00:08:19,801 --> 00:08:21,867
Four were rated very strange.

123
00:08:21,867 --> 00:08:26,000
The strangest, Strand said, was a red light on the ground.

124
00:08:26,466 --> 00:08:28,105
The mystery did not stop.

125
00:08:28,105 --> 00:08:35,905
In nineteen ninety-seven and ninety-eight, volunteers recorded a pyramid-shaped cluster of lights bouncing up and down.

126
00:08:35,905 --> 00:08:42,133
And in nineteen ninety-eight, a permanent Automatic Measurement Station was built on the slope.

127
00:08:42,600 --> 00:08:49,694
A box on a hill with cameras, a magnetometer, weather sensors and radio receivers, switched on day and night.

128
00:08:49,694 --> 00:08:56,333
It was built to answer plain questions: do the lights keep coming, and what else changes when they do?

129
00:08:56,799 --> 00:09:00,938
The logs say the lights usually show between nine p.m.

130
00:09:00,938 --> 00:09:03,926
and one a.m., and more often in winter.

131
00:09:03,926 --> 00:09:08,678
By twenty-ten, sightings had dwindled to ten or twenty a year.

132
00:09:08,678 --> 00:09:11,666
The peak of twenty a week is long gone.

133
00:09:12,133 --> 00:09:14,488
Then came an international team.

134
00:09:14,488 --> 00:09:28,766
Between two thousand and two thousand two, Italy's national research council and Norway's Østfold University College ran expeditions under the name EMBLA, watching with cameras, radio and radar.

135
00:09:29,233 --> 00:09:38,834
Astrophysicist Massimo Teodorani, one of the Italians, calculated that a big light ball could radiate up to nineteen kilowatts.

136
00:09:38,834 --> 00:09:43,899
Balls ranged from tens of centimeters to some thirty meters across.

137
00:09:44,366 --> 00:09:48,562
EMBLA's conclusion: the balls are not single solid objects.

138
00:09:48,562 --> 00:09:58,233
They look like clusters of small parts vibrating around a center, sometimes throwing off smaller balls, and changing shape all the time.

139
00:09:58,700 --> 00:10:01,439
That is hard to match with a machine.

140
00:10:01,439 --> 00:10:02,845
A craft has a hull.

141
00:10:02,845 --> 00:10:08,250
These show no hull, no edges, no wings, only glow that shifts and splits.

142
00:10:08,250 --> 00:10:12,766
For the alien theory, the best data is also the most awkward.

143
00:10:13,233 --> 00:10:19,517
A scientists' survey in Frontiers in Earth Science, in twenty-sixteen, put it plainly.

144
00:10:19,517 --> 00:10:26,312
No model explains every observation, and the energy source behind the lights remains unknown.

145
00:10:26,312 --> 00:10:28,066
So who are the suspects?

146
00:10:28,533 --> 00:10:30,552
Suspect one: burning dust.

147
00:10:30,552 --> 00:10:32,805
The valley has a mining past.

148
00:10:32,805 --> 00:10:42,281
In two thousand seven, engineer Bjørn Hauge proposed that scandium-rich dust ignites in air and drifts as a glowing cloud.

149
00:10:42,281 --> 00:10:45,466
The press ran a headline: mystery solved.

150
00:10:45,933 --> 00:10:49,722
It was never proven, and the headline was premature.

151
00:10:49,722 --> 00:10:52,419
Its own authors call it a hypothesis.

152
00:10:52,419 --> 00:10:56,500
But it fits a hot continuous glow, and the dust is real.

153
00:10:56,966 --> 00:10:58,472
Suspect two: radon.

154
00:10:58,472 --> 00:11:14,166
In twenty-ten, physicists Gerson Paiva and Carlton Taft proposed that radioactive radon in the rock ionizes air and dust, forming a dusty plasma whose clumps glow and arrange themselves in patterns.

155
00:11:14,633 --> 00:11:20,626
A neat feature: the model predicts shapes and oscillations that people have photographed.

156
00:11:20,626 --> 00:11:22,781
The weakness: it lives on paper.

157
00:11:22,781 --> 00:11:28,033
As far as I can find, nobody has measured such a crystal in the valley itself.

158
00:11:28,500 --> 00:11:30,404
Suspect three: squeezed rock.

159
00:11:30,404 --> 00:11:32,572
Quartz makes charge under strain.

160
00:11:32,572 --> 00:11:44,066
But skeptic Brian Dunning notes the valley is mostly schist and sandstone with little quartz, and the lights are seen from a hill at the north end, not rising from the ground.

161
00:11:44,533 --> 00:11:46,914
Suspect four: a natural battery.

162
00:11:46,914 --> 00:12:00,533
In twenty-fourteen, Italian engineer Jader Monari suggested the two sides of the valley act as electrodes and the river Hesja as the electrolyte, so charged gas bubbles rise and glow.

163
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:05,001
Suspect five is the simplest and least glamorous: aircraft.

164
00:12:05,001 --> 00:12:14,700
Skeptoid's Brian Dunning cites a published warning from researcher Marsha Adams that airplane lights are a common source of false ghost lights.

165
00:12:15,166 --> 00:12:24,384
A flight corridor from the Tolga navigation beacon runs straight up the valley, toward the station, on the bearing its cameras face.

166
00:12:24,384 --> 00:12:27,736
Oslo to Trondheim is Norway's busiest air route.

167
00:12:27,736 --> 00:12:32,066
Most station images, Dunning argues, look like landing lights.

168
00:12:32,533 --> 00:12:35,510
Aircraft may explain many modern photographs.

169
00:12:35,510 --> 00:12:45,299
They do not obviously explain reports from the nineteen thirties, before regular flights, or a flashing light that changed its rhythm under a laser.

170
00:12:45,766 --> 00:12:48,130
Here is the uncomfortable truth.

171
00:12:48,130 --> 00:12:54,114
No single theory covers everything: not dust, not plasma, not rock, not aircraft.

172
00:12:54,114 --> 00:13:02,166
My reading: ordinary lights mistaken for something strange, plus a rarer natural glow nobody yet understands.

173
00:13:02,633 --> 00:13:05,196
Notice who never needed to be named.

174
00:13:05,196 --> 00:13:09,896
Four decades of instruments found glow, echoes and a flash rhythm.

175
00:13:09,896 --> 00:13:15,166
They did not find a hull, a message, or any technology you could point to.

176
00:13:15,633 --> 00:13:18,602
I want to give the alien idea its best case.

177
00:13:18,602 --> 00:13:24,000
The lights hover, cluster, travel in lines, and once seemed to react to a laser.

178
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:29,533
If you designed a probe to look like glowing plasma, this is what you would build.

179
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:31,746
The trouble is the logic.

180
00:13:31,746 --> 00:13:39,362
Everything that fits a probe also fits an unknown natural glow, and the natural glow needs fewer assumptions.

181
00:13:39,362 --> 00:13:44,533
When two explanations fit equally, take the simpler one and keep watching.

182
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:46,486
Time to score the case.

183
00:13:46,486 --> 00:13:49,200
Evidence on one side, theory on the other.

184
00:13:49,666 --> 00:13:54,726
Lights over the valley for decades, recorded by instruments: evidence.

185
00:13:54,726 --> 00:13:58,050
Radar echoes as strong as mountains: evidence.

186
00:13:58,050 --> 00:14:04,266
A continuous spectrum and a laser response: evidence, of something we cannot yet name.

187
00:14:04,733 --> 00:14:08,939
Burning dust or natural plasma: plausible, unproven.

188
00:14:08,939 --> 00:14:13,470
Aircraft lights: likely for some modern photos, not all.

189
00:14:13,470 --> 00:14:17,595
An alien craft: no hull, no message, no technology.

190
00:14:17,595 --> 00:14:18,566
No evidence.

191
00:14:19,033 --> 00:14:19,956
The verdict.

192
00:14:19,956 --> 00:14:20,803
Not aliens.

193
00:14:20,803 --> 00:14:29,500
Hessdalen is a genuine, partly unexplained natural light phenomenon, mixed with ordinary aircraft and car lights.

194
00:14:29,500 --> 00:14:33,733
The unexplained part is real science, not a spacecraft.

195
00:14:34,200 --> 00:14:42,768
And that is the better story: a valley where physicists still argue about what is glowing, with a station watching every night.

196
00:14:42,768 --> 00:14:48,166
The answer, when it comes, will likely be strange plasma physics, not a landing.

197
00:14:48,633 --> 00:14:58,826
Next case: beneath a pyramid city in Mexico, archaeologists entered a tunnel sealed for eighteen hundred years, and found liquid mercury inside.

198
00:14:58,826 --> 00:15:01,799
Ancient power plant, or sacred underworld?

199
00:15:02,266 --> 00:15:06,268
Which suspect do you trust: dust, plasma, rock or aircraft?

200
00:15:06,268 --> 00:15:11,966
Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for the next ancient mystery put to the test.
